  — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m uses a plain [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m (no module system at all) and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m.
  There's no format that both consume as-is, so sharing a module forces a choice:

  - [1mESM everywhere[0m — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gets [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m, [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m/[38;5;153mutils.js[39m convert [38;5;153mrequire[39m→[38;5;153mimport[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m
    becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. Native in both runtimes, no build step, and it's the format any future tooling will
    assume. Cost: it touches [38;5;153mutils.js[39m and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, which are otherwise unrelated to logging.
  - [1mDual-format wrapper (UMD-style)[0m — one file that detects [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m and otherwise attaches to [38;5;153mwindow[39m. Touches
    nothing existing. Cost: a dated pattern, awkward to test, and it tends to be the thing you rip out the first time
    you add a bundler.
  - [1mTwo thin adapters over a shared core[0m — core logic in one file, a CommonJS shim and a browser-global shim. Honest but
    the most code for a project this size.

  This one is genuinely hard to change later — the format is baked into every import site — which is why it's worth a
  minute now.

  [1mMy recommendation: ESM everywhere.[0m The conversion here is about six lines across three files, and it's the only option
  that doesn't leave a legacy pattern in a brand-new module.

  On tooling: this repo has no test runner, no linter, and no scripts. A redaction rule with no test asserting that
  passwords don't appear in output is a rule that quietly stops holding. Setting this up is cheapest now, before the
  module exists.
